"""Distractors, part 1. Realistic tribal knowledge, never gold for any query.

These exist so top-5 retrieval is a genuine test. They are deliberately drawn from the SAME
topical space as the traps (builds, tests, env vars, migrations, timeouts, caches, TLS,
serialisation) so they compete for the same queries. A corpus of off-topic filler would
inflate every number.

Format: (key, gist, content).
"""

_D = [
("d-npm-lock", "The lock file must be committed from a clean install or transitive versions drift between machines.",
 "Running the install with an existing partial tree produces a lock file that pins different transitive versions than a clean install does. The drift shows up as a test passing on one machine and failing on another. Delete the tree and reinstall before committing a lock change."),
("d-node-heap", "Node builds need the heap size raised explicitly or the bundler dies late in the build with no clear message.",
 "The bundler exceeds the default heap on the largest entry point. It dies with an abort trace that does not name memory. Set the max-old-space option in the build script rather than in a developer's shell so CI gets it too."),
("d-pytest-order", "Test ordering is randomised by default in this repo, so a suite that only passes in file order is hiding a dependency.",
 "The runner is configured to shuffle within a module. If a test only passes in declaration order it is depending on state left by a neighbour. Do not disable the shuffle to make it pass; fix the shared state."),
("d-coverage-gate", "The coverage gate measures only changed lines, so a large refactor can drop total coverage and still pass.",
 "The gate is diff-scoped. That is deliberate, but it means total coverage can decline while every individual change passes. Watch the trend chart, not the per-PR verdict."),
("d-docker-buildkit", "The build requires the newer builder backend; with the legacy backend the cache mounts are silently ignored.",
 "Cache mount directives are a no-op on the legacy builder, so the build succeeds but takes several times longer with no warning. Export the builder selection variable in CI explicitly."),
("d-tls-1-0", "The partner's older endpoint still requires the deprecated TLS version, so it must be reached through the proxy.",
 "One partner endpoint has not moved off the deprecated protocol version, which our default client refuses. Route those calls through the compatibility proxy rather than weakening the client's minimum version globally."),
("d-log-sampling", "Application logs are sampled at one in ten above a rate threshold, so a missing log line does not prove the code did not run.",
 "The logging pipeline samples aggressively once a service exceeds a per-second threshold. People conclude a branch never executed because its log line is absent. Use a counter metric for anything you need to reason about reliably."),
("d-trace-sampling", "Traces are head-sampled at one percent, so most requests have no trace and searching for a specific request usually fails.",
 "Head sampling at one percent means a given request almost certainly has no trace. Tail sampling was discussed and not implemented. For a specific investigation, enable the per-request debug header, which forces capture."),
("d-metric-cardinality", "Adding a label with unbounded values to a metric will get the metric dropped by the collector's cardinality guard.",
 "The collector enforces a series-per-metric ceiling and drops the whole metric when exceeded, not just the new series. A metric silently disappearing from dashboards is usually this. Keep labels to bounded enumerations."),
("d-alert-flap", "The latency alert flaps during deploys because the rollout briefly serves from fewer instances; it is not actionable.",
 "During a rolling deploy the remaining instances take extra load and p99 crosses the alert threshold for a minute or two. It self-resolves. The alert has not been given a deploy-aware inhibition rule yet, so it is routinely ignored, which is its own problem."),
("d-secret-rotation", "Rotating the signing key requires both keys to be trusted for one full token lifetime, or in-flight tokens are rejected.",
 "The verifier must accept old and new keys during the overlap, which is the token lifetime plus a margin. Cutting straight over invalidates every token issued before the switch. The rotation script does the overlap; doing it by hand usually does not."),
("d-kms-region", "The encryption key is region-pinned, so a failover to the secondary region cannot decrypt existing payloads.",
 "The key exists only in the primary region. A regional failover leaves the secondary unable to decrypt anything already stored. This is a known gap in the disaster-recovery story and is documented as accepted risk rather than fixed."),
("d-iam-eventual", "Newly granted permissions take up to a minute to become effective, so a permission test immediately after granting is unreliable.",
 "Policy propagation is eventually consistent. A script that grants and then immediately exercises the permission will fail intermittently. Add a retry loop around the first authorised call rather than a fixed sleep."),
("d-s3-list-cost", "Listing the artefact bucket without a prefix walks millions of keys and costs real money; always pass a prefix.",
 "The artefact bucket has millions of objects. An unprefixed list is slow and billed per request page. A cleanup script that did this once produced a noticeable line item. Always scope with a prefix and a max-keys bound."),
("d-cdn-invalidate", "CDN invalidation is not instant and is billed per path, so bulk invalidation after a deploy is the wrong pattern.",
 "Invalidations take minutes to propagate and are charged per path above a monthly free allowance. Deploys should change the asset URL instead, which makes invalidation unnecessary. The build already emits content-hashed names for this reason."),
("d-queue-visibility", "The queue's visibility timeout is shorter than the worst-case handler duration, so slow messages get delivered twice.",
 "A handler that exceeds the visibility timeout has its message redelivered while still processing, producing duplicate side effects. Handlers must be idempotent, and the two that are not are known and tracked. Extending the timeout has knock-on effects on redrive."),
("d-dlq-silent", "The dead-letter queue has no alarm on it, so poison messages accumulate unnoticed.",
 "Messages that exhaust their receive count land in the dead-letter queue and nothing watches it. A batch of malformed events sat there for three weeks. An alarm on queue depth is the obvious fix and has not been done."),
("d-idempotency-key", "The idempotency key is derived from the payload hash, so a retry with a reordered map produces a different key.",
 "Because the key is a hash of the serialised payload and map ordering is not stable across languages, a retry from a different client can produce a different key and duplicate the effect. Sort keys before hashing; the Python client does, the other one does not."),
("d-clock-monotonic", "Duration measurement must use the monotonic clock; the wall clock steps backwards during synchronisation and produces negative durations.",
 "Negative durations in the metrics come from measuring with the wall clock across a time synchronisation step. Use the monotonic clock for all interval measurement. Several older call sites still do not."),
("d-thread-fork", "Forking after threads have started leaves locks held in the child, which deadlocks on the first allocation.",
 "The worker pool forks. If any library has already started a thread that holds an internal lock, the child inherits the locked state and hangs immediately. Start the pool before anything else, and never fork lazily on first use."),
("d-signal-handler", "The graceful-shutdown handler is registered too late, so a fast rollout kills the process before it can drain.",
 "The termination handler is installed after the server begins accepting connections, so a signal arriving in that window is handled by the default disposition and the process dies without draining. Register the handler first."),
("d-readiness-vs-liveness", "The readiness and liveness probes point at the same endpoint, so a slow dependency causes restarts instead of removal from the pool.",
 "Both probes hit one endpoint that checks the downstream dependency. When the dependency is slow, liveness fails and the orchestrator restarts a healthy process. Split them: liveness should check only the process, readiness may check dependencies."),
("d-hpa-thrash", "The autoscaler's scale-down window is shorter than the pod start time, so load spikes cause oscillation.",
 "Scale-down happens faster than new instances can become ready, so the autoscaler removes capacity it is about to need. Lengthening the stabilisation window fixed it; changing the target utilisation did not."),
("d-node-taint", "The GPU node pool is tainted, so a workload without the matching toleration schedules on CPU nodes and runs a hundred times slower.",
 "There is no error when the toleration is missing. The pod schedules on a general node and the workload falls back to CPU. It looks like a performance regression rather than a scheduling mistake. Assert on the device count at start."),
("d-configmap-reload", "Config map changes are not picked up without a restart, because the mount is read once at start.",
 "The process reads the mounted config at start and caches it. Editing the config map updates the file on disk within a minute but nothing rereads it. A rollout restart is required; there is no watch."),
("d-initcontainer-order", "The init container that runs migrations will retry forever if the database is unreachable, so a bad connection string looks like a slow start.",
 "The migration init container has no failure ceiling and retries indefinitely, so a typo in the connection string presents as a pod stuck in initialisation with no obvious error. Check the init container logs specifically; the main container has none yet."),
("d-pvc-resize", "Volume expansion works online but shrinking does not, so over-provisioning is a one-way decision.",
 "Volumes can be grown in place without downtime. They cannot be shrunk at all; the only path is create-new-and-copy. Size conservatively at first."),
("d-cron-overlap", "The nightly job has no concurrency guard, so a run that overruns into the next window doubles up.",
 "When the nightly job takes longer than its interval, a second instance starts alongside it. The two then contend on the same rows and both slow down, which makes the overlap worse. A lease-based guard is the fix; the scheduler's own concurrency policy was not honoured in this deployment."),
("d-tz-cron", "The scheduler runs in UTC, so a job specified for local business hours drifts by an hour twice a year.",
 "Schedules are evaluated in UTC with no zone support. A job intended for local morning runs an hour early or late after a daylight change. Either accept the drift or compute the schedule from a zone-aware generator."),
("d-leapsecond", "The time library used by the report boundary does not handle the repeated second, which double-counts one row per occurrence.",
 "A repeated second produces two rows with identical timestamps and the boundary logic counts both into each window. It is a once-in-years event and the impact is one row, so it has been documented rather than fixed."),
("d-float-money", "Monetary amounts are stored as integer minor units; any code that converts to float for arithmetic will produce off-by-one totals.",
 "Amounts are integers in minor units precisely to avoid representation error. A helper that converted to float for a percentage calculation reintroduced it and produced totals off by a unit. Do percentage maths in integers with explicit rounding."),
("d-decimal-context", "The decimal context precision is set globally at import, so a library that changes it affects unrelated calculations.",
 "One module sets the global decimal context at import time. Anything imported after it inherits the change, which has produced surprising rounding in unrelated code. Use a local context manager instead."),
("d-sort-locale", "String sorting depends on the process locale, so ordering differs between the container and a developer machine.",
 "Sort order for non-ASCII strings follows the locale, which is unset in the container and set on most laptops. A test asserting on order therefore passes locally and fails in CI. Sort with an explicit key rather than relying on default collation."),
("d-regex-catastrophic", "The validation regex backtracks catastrophically on a long input, which is a denial-of-service path on a public endpoint.",
 "One nested-quantifier pattern takes exponential time on adversarial input. On the public endpoint that is a denial-of-service. It was replaced with an explicit parser. Do not reintroduce nested quantifiers in request validation."),
("d-yaml-anchors", "Anchors and aliases in the config are expanded by the loader but not by the schema validator, so validated config differs from loaded config.",
 "The validator runs on the raw document and sees the alias node; the loader expands it. A config can therefore pass validation and load differently. Avoid anchors in files that are validated."),
("d-toml-multiline", "Multi-line strings in the config preserve leading whitespace, which breaks the embedded query when the config is reindented.",
 "An embedded query is stored as a multi-line string. Reindenting the config file changes the string content and, for one query, changed its meaning. Move embedded queries to their own files."),
("d-env-quotes", "Quotes in the environment file are taken literally by the runtime but stripped by the shell loader, so behaviour differs by launch path.",
 "Launching through the shell wrapper strips quotes; launching the binary directly with the file loaded by the runtime does not. A value with quotes therefore differs by launch path. Do not quote values in that file."),
("d-path-length", "The build fails on the Windows runner only, because the intermediate object paths exceed the path length limit.",
 "Deeply nested intermediate directories exceed the platform path limit on one runner. The failure names a file that exists, which is confusing. Shorten the build output directory; long-path support was enabled and did not help for this toolchain."),
("d-case-insensitive-fs", "Two files differing only in case exist in the repo, which breaks checkout on case-insensitive filesystems.",
 "A rename that changed only case left two paths in history. Checkout on a case-insensitive filesystem collides and reports a dirty tree immediately after clone. The tracked fix is to rename one via a two-step change."),
("d-gitignore-tracked", "A file already tracked is not affected by adding it to the ignore file, which is why the local config keeps getting committed.",
 "Ignore rules only apply to untracked paths. The local config was committed once, so it continues to be tracked and shows up in every diff. It needs an explicit index removal, which will delete it for everyone on next pull unless they save a copy."),
("d-submodule-detached", "Submodules check out a detached commit, so edits made inside one are easy to lose.",
 "A submodule is at a commit, not a branch. Work done inside it without first checking out a branch is on no branch and is lost on the next update. Check out a branch inside the submodule before editing."),
("d-lfs-missing", "Large files are stored out of band and a clone without the extension installed yields pointer files that fail at runtime with a parse error.",
 "Without the extension, the working tree contains small text pointer files where binaries should be. The failure is a parse error in whatever reads them, which does not suggest the real cause. Install the extension and re-fetch."),
("d-shallow-clone", "The CI clone is shallow, so anything that reads history further back than one commit fails only in CI.",
 "The default CI checkout fetches a single commit. Version derivation from tags, and any diff against the merge base, therefore fail or produce wrong answers. Ask for the deeper fetch explicitly in the job that needs it."),
("d-merge-commit-ci", "CI builds the merge result, not your branch tip, so a green branch can fail after an unrelated change lands on the target.",
 "The pipeline builds the merge of your branch with the current target. A failure can therefore be caused by something that landed on the target after your last push. Re-run before assuming your change is at fault."),
("d-rebase-hooks", "The pre-push hook runs the full suite, which makes an interactive rebase with many commits take a very long time.",
 "The hook runs on every pushed commit rather than once per push, so a rebase that rewrites twenty commits runs the suite twenty times. There is a documented environment variable to run it once; it is not the default."),
("d-tag-immutable", "Release tags are protected and cannot be moved, so a bad release requires a new patch tag rather than a retag.",
 "Tag protection is on. A mistake in a release requires cutting the next patch version. Attempting to force-update a tag fails at the server, which is intentional and has saved us at least once."),
("d-changelog-gen", "The changelog is generated from commit subjects, so a squash merge with a generic subject loses the detail.",
 "Generation reads commit subjects on the target branch. A squash merge collapses everything into one subject, so a descriptive body is not picked up. Write the subject as the changelog line you want."),
("d-semver-zero", "Dependencies in the pre-one version range are treated as breaking on every minor bump by the resolver, so ranges do not do what you expect there.",
 "For versions below one, the resolver treats the minor position as the breaking position. A caret range therefore does not span minor versions. Two of our dependencies are in that range and must be pinned exactly."),
("d-transitive-pin", "Pinning a transitive dependency requires an override entry; adding it as a direct dependency does not constrain the transitive resolution.",
 "Adding the package as a direct dependency at the wanted version leaves the transitive resolution free to pick another, and the loader may get either. The override mechanism is the only reliable way. This has bitten us twice."),
("d-native-wheel", "One dependency has no wheel for the runtime version we use, so it compiles from source and adds several minutes to every cold install.",
 "The cold install compiles that package because no matching wheel is published. It is a few minutes each time and is the main reason the dependency cache matters. A prebuilt wheel is published internally now but the index priority has not been changed to prefer it."),
("d-pip-resolver", "The installer will silently pick an older version to satisfy a conflict rather than failing, which is how a very old library ended up installed.",
 "Given an unsatisfiable constraint set the resolver backtracks to an old version that does satisfy it, and reports success. A library several major versions behind was installed this way and only noticed when an expected function was missing. Read the resolved versions after any dependency change."),
("d-editable-install", "An editable install shadows the packaged version in a way that hides missing package data files until deployment.",
 "Under an editable install, data files are read from the source tree whether or not they are declared for packaging. Anything undeclared works in development and is missing in the built artefact. Build the artefact and test against it before release."),
("d-namespace-package", "Two distributions contribute to the same namespace, and installing one without the other produces a partially populated package with confusing import errors.",
 "The namespace is split across two distributions. Installing only one leaves imports of the other's modules failing with an error that names the namespace, not the missing distribution. Both are required and neither declares the other."),
("d-import-side-effect", "Importing the client module opens a connection as a side effect, which makes it unsafe to import at module scope in the worker.",
 "The client module connects at import. In the forking worker that means a connection is created pre-fork and shared, which produces interleaved protocol errors. Import it inside the function, or use the lazy factory."),
("d-circular-import", "The two modules import each other and only work because one import is inside a function; moving it to the top breaks start-up.",
 "There is a genuine cycle, worked around by a function-scoped import. A well-intentioned tidy-up that hoisted it to the top produced an import error at start. The cycle should be broken properly but the refactor is large."),
("d-abstract-registry", "Handlers register themselves on import, so a handler in a module nobody imports is silently absent.",
 "Registration is a decorator side effect. If the module is never imported the handler does not exist and requests for it fall through to the default with no error. The package's init imports them all explicitly for this reason; add new ones there."),
("d-enum-serialization", "Enum values are serialised by name in one path and by ordinal in another, so reordering the enum breaks stored data.",
 "One serialiser writes the name, the other writes the position. Inserting a value in the middle therefore invalidates anything stored by the second. Append new values at the end only, until the ordinal path is removed."),
("d-bool-env", "The environment parser treats any non-empty string as true, so setting a flag to the string false enables it.",
 "There is no truthiness parsing; presence and non-emptiness are the test. Setting a flag to the literal text false turns it on, which has caught several people. Unset the variable to disable."),
("d-null-vs-absent", "The API distinguishes an explicit null from an absent field, and the client's default serialiser omits nulls, so clearing a field is impossible through it.",
 "Clearing a field requires sending an explicit null. The client omits null fields by default, so a clear is indistinguishable from no change. Enable the include-nulls option on that specific call."),
]

MEMORIES = [dict(key=k, cat="distractor", gist=g, content=c) for (k, g, c) in _D]
